    95	```bash
    96	# ntfy.sh example
    97	export DEVFLOW_GATE_NOTIFY_CMD='curl -s -d "devflow gate: phase $DEVFLOW_GATE_PHASE $DEVFLOW_GATE_STAGE — $DEVFLOW_GATE_CONTEXT" ntfy.sh/my-topic'
    98	```
    99	
   100	Env provided to the hook: `DEVFLOW_GATE_PHASE`, `DEVFLOW_GATE_STAGE`,
   101	`DEVFLOW_GATE_CONTEXT`, `DEVFLOW_NON_SILENT_GATE` (`1` when the gate exists
   102	only because a stage failed unexpectedly).
   103	
   104	## Environment variables
   105	
   106	| Variable | Default | Purpose |
   107	|---|---|---|
   108	| `DEVFLOW_GATE_NOTIFY_CMD` | unset | Shell command fired when a gate is written |
   109	| `DEVFLOW_GATE_TIMEOUT_SECS` | 259200 (3d) | How long a monitor stays alive holding the phase lock while parked at a gate. Not a deadline for answering: the gate request and phase state are files that never expire, and timing out is a clean resumable stop — past it you answer the gate and run `devflow resume` instead of the answer being picked up automatically |
   110	| `DEVFLOW_FOREGROUND_GATE_TIMEOUT_SECS` | 60 | How long `devflow ship --phase`'s foreground manual override waits for a reopened Ship gate (terminal-hook failure) before failing fast, instead of `DEVFLOW_GATE_TIMEOUT_SECS`' multi-day default |
   111	| `DEVFLOW_CHECKOUT_LOCK_TIMEOUT_SECS` | 120 | Wait on the shared-checkout lock; on timeout the hook batch is skipped (loudly), never run unserialized |
   112	| `DEVFLOW_GATE_MAX_UNATTENDED_AGE_SECS` | 259200 (3d) | Age threshold `devflow gate sweep` uses to decide a gate is abandoned. Held **equal to** `DEVFLOW_GATE_TIMEOUT_SECS`, never shorter: `gate sweep` writes an `abort:` response, so a threshold below the poll timeout lets a sweep reap gates a live monitor is still polling — turning a clean resumable timeout into an `abort()` that clears state, machine-wide across every registered root. An unparsable value or explicit `0` falls back to the default rather than reaping every gate on the machine |
   113	| `DEVFLOW_CACHE_DIR` | unset (falls back to `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/devflow`, then `$HOME/.cache/devflow`) | Test/override hook for the machine-global registry directory (`devflow gate list --all-roots`) |
   114	| `DEVFLOW_CAPTURE_RETENTION` | 5 | Capture generations retained per phase; overrides `devflow.toml` |
   115	| `DEVFLOW_REVIEW_ANGLES` | built-in five-angle list | Comma-separated Ship review angles; overrides `devflow.toml` |
   116	| `DEVFLOW_EXTERNAL_VERIFY_ENABLED` | true | Enable PLAN-declared external post-condition probes; overrides `devflow.toml` |
   117	| `DEVFLOW_YES_SHIP` | false | Standing Ship gate pre-authorization (D-12, `28-CONTEXT.md`); overrides `devflow.toml`'s `yes_ship` key, which itself is OR-combined with `--yes-ship` at `devflow start` |
   118	| `DEVFLOW_ANTIGRAVITY_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECS` | 120 | The per-agent idle timeout for the pipe-owning Antigravity monitor (phase 41, D-08): how long the monitor waits with NO output on the child's `stream-json` stream before recording an `idle_timeout` verdict and terminating it. Same 120s floor and clamp semantics as `DEVFLOW_CLAUDE_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECS`. The default is a DECIDED starting point, not a silent inheritance of the Claude-measured floor — it is to be revisited after the first real Antigravity cadence measurement. Claude's variable is never read for Antigravity and vice versa |
   119	| `DEVFLOW_CLAUDE_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECS` | 120 | How long the pipe-owning Claude monitor waits with NO output on the child's stream before recording an `idle_timeout` verdict and terminating it. Clamped to a 120s floor: a lower value is raised and the clamp is printed loudly at `devflow start`. The floor is 4x the CLI's measured `tool_progress` keepalive interval (a fixed 30.00s, measured 2026-08-03 over five workload-controlled trials on CLI 2.1.220), so it survives three consecutive missed keepalives; the earlier 30s floor sat exactly ON the keepalive interval and would have killed healthy stages running any tool call longer than ~30s, including `cargo test --workspace`. Every stream line resets the window, so a healthy long-running stage is never bounded by wall clock |
   120	| `DEVFLOW_CLAUDE_LEGACY_LAUNCH` | false | D-11's escape hatch (31-04): force the pre-31 single-document Claude launch — prompt positionally in argv, `--output-format json`, the `sh` monitor — instead of the Phase 31 `stream-json` transport. OR-combined with `--legacy-claude-launch` at `devflow start` / `devflow resume` and persisted to `state-NN.json`, so a detached monitor honours it; a run authorized by this variable alone prints a notice naming it as the source. **Parsed as a bool: `=false` does NOT enable it, and an unparseable value warns and leaves the legacy path OFF.** Using it is announced on three channels (stdout, `phase-NN-monitor.log`, and a `claude_legacy_launch_forced` event in `events.jsonl`) because an escape hatch used routinely erodes what it protects. **What it gives up: the legacy path cannot deliver background-task notifications, so a multi-plan wave may orphan delegated work — that is 999.64, the defect Phase 31 exists to fix.** Once set for a run it is never cleared by `devflow resume`; edit `state-NN.json` to turn it off |
   121	| `RUST_LOG` | `info` | Log verbosity (stderr) |
   122	| `DEVFLOW_LOG_FORMAT` | plain | `json` for machine-readable log lines |
   123	| `DEVFLOW_E2E_CHILD_TIMEOUT_SECS` | 90 | Test-only: bounds `gate_sweep_e2e.rs`'s patience with a spawned `devflow advance` child so CI cannot hang indefinitely; not read by any production code path |
   124	
   125	## `.devflow/` file inventory
   126	
   127	| File | What it is |
   128	|---|---|
   129	| `state-NN.json` | A phase's persisted stage-machine state (per phase since 14a) |
   130	| `lock-NN` | Per-phase advance lock (held across gate waits) |
   131	| `lock-project` | Coarse checkout lock (held seconds, around shared-git mutations) |
   132	| `phase-NN-stdout` / `phase-NN-stderr.log` | Agent capture files (what `logs` tails) |
   133	| `phase-NN-exit` / `phase-NN-agent-pid` | Exit code + PID the monitor records |
   134	| `gates/NN-<stage>.json` (+ `.response.json`, `.ack.json`) | Gate request / answer / receipt |
   135	| `events.jsonl` | Append-only event log (schema v1, one JSON object per line, phase id on every line) — tail it from any tool |
     1	# Unattended Mode
     2	
     3	`devflow start --mode auto` is for the run you start and walk away from. This
     4	page is what you should read before you do that: what the mode actually
     5	automates, what DevFlow checks before it will start one, what it writes into
     6	your git history, and the two places an unattended run can still stop.
     7	
     8	## What unattended mode does
     9	
    10	For the duration of the **Code** stage — and of the `--gaps-only` fix loop that
    11	a failing Validate sends back to Code — DevFlow sets GSD's chain flag,
    12	`workflow._auto_chain_active`, in your project's `.planning/config.json`. GSD
    13	reads that flag when a plan reaches an ordinary blocking checkpoint. With the
    14	flag set, GSD approves the checkpoint and keeps going instead of stopping to
    15	ask you.
    16	
    17	The flag is set when the stage launches and cleared when it finishes, on every
    18	exit path including a failure.
    19	
    20	**Name the boundary plainly: this is checkpoint approval only.** Two things it
    21	is deliberately *not*:
    22	
    23	- **It is not stage-chaining.** DevFlow still drives the pipeline itself,
    24	  one stage at a time. GSD is never told to launch the next stage on its own.
    25	- **It never writes `workflow.auto_advance`.** That is your persistent
    26	  settings preference, and DevFlow does not touch it. Only the ephemeral
    27	  `_auto_chain_active` key is ever written, and only that one key.
    28	
    29	Checkpoints marked `blocking-human`, and `checkpoint:human-action` tasks, are
    30	never auto-approved by any mode. A phase whose plans declare one is refused
    31	before it starts — see the next section.
    32	
    33	## Before you start: what the preflight checks
    34	
    35	An unattended launch is checked before any agent is spawned. Three conditions
    36	are evaluated and **all three are reported every time**, in both `auto` and
    37	`supervise` mode, so you can rehearse a run in supervise and see exactly what
    38	an auto run would say. Only `--mode auto` is refused on a failure.
    39	
    40	Each condition reports one of `[holds]`, `[DOES NOT HOLD]`,
    41	`[COULD NOT BE DETERMINED]`, or `[not yet applicable]`. In auto mode, anything
    42	that is not a definite pass refuses — including *could not determine*.
    43	Unreadable is not the same as absent, and neither is treated as fine.
    44	
    45	### 1. The GSD config can hold the chain flag
    46	
    47	`.planning/config.json` must exist under the launch root, parse, and be a JSON
    48	object. If it is missing there is nowhere for the flag to live; if it does not
    49	parse, DevFlow cannot tell whether writing to it would destroy something.
    50	
    51	A refusal here means your project is not GSD-configured, or its config file is
    52	malformed. Fix the file, or run in supervise mode.
    53	
    54	### 2. The Code stage would launch on the pipe-owning arm
    55	
    56	The guard that bounds the flag's lifetime lives inside DevFlow's pipe-owning
    57	monitor, and only the Claude launch path starts one. So two launch shapes are
    58	refused in auto mode:
    59	
    60	- **A non-Claude agent.** `--agent codex` and `--agent opencode` cannot run
    61	  `--mode auto`. This is a real capability removal introduced with this
    62	  feature; those agents still work in supervise mode.
    63	- **The legacy launch opt-out.** `--legacy-claude-launch` (or
    64	  `DEVFLOW_CLAUDE_LEGACY_LAUNCH`) selects a detached shell monitor with no
    65	  process to bound the flag's lifetime. It is now mutually exclusive with
    66	  `--mode auto`.
    67	
    68	The recovery for either is the same: drop the flag that caused it, or run in
    69	supervise mode.
    70	
    71	### 3. No plan declares a checkpoint GSD never auto-approves
    72	
    73	DevFlow scans the phase's `*-PLAN.md` files for a `blocking-human` gate or a
    74	`checkpoint:human-action` task declared on a task tag. Those are human-only by
    75	design and no mode approves them, so an unattended run that reached one would
    76	simply stall. Better to say so at the start.
    77	
    78	Before the phase has been planned there is nothing to scan, and the condition
    79	reports `[not yet applicable]` at Define rather than refusing. At Code, plans
    80	are expected — their absence is `[COULD NOT BE DETERMINED]`, and that refuses.
    81	
    82	A refusal here means replanning the phase without the human-only marker, or
    83	running in supervise mode and answering it yourself.
    84	
    85	### A refusal is final
    86	
    87	There is no `--force-unattended` flag and no environment variable that turns a
    88	refusal into a warning. Your options are to fix the condition or to run in
    89	supervise mode.
    90	
    91	**Both external review lanes objected to that, on record, and the decision
    92	stands.** The objection is real and worth stating so you know it was heard: a
    93	preflight that false-positives makes unattended runs impossible with no
    94	in-product recovery, and one reviewer characterised that as a
    95	denial-of-service. The counter-argument that carried is DevFlow's standing
    96	principle — advance as far as the rules permit and stop at the first hard gate,
    97	never route around one — plus the asymmetry that adding an override later is
    98	easy while removing one after operators have built habits around it is not. If
    99	the preflight's own reliability turns out to be a problem in practice, that is
   100	grounds to reopen the decision, not to work around it.
   101	
   102	## What you will see in your git history
   103	
   104	The chain flag lives in `.planning/config.json`, which is a **tracked** file.
   105	So:
   106	
   107	- **During the Code stage, that file is modified in your working tree.** The
   108	  value flips to `true` when the stage launches and back to `false` when it
   109	  finishes. Only that one key changes — key order, formatting, and every other
   110	  setting are preserved byte-for-byte.
   111	
   112	- **If a run is killed, the flag can be left set.** A `SIGKILL` gives DevFlow's
   113	  in-process guard no chance to run. The next `devflow start` or
   114	  `devflow resume` for that phase detects the leftover value and clears it
   115	  before launching anything, printing a notice as it does.
   116	
   117	- **That repair may write a commit.** If the leaked `true` had already reached
   118	  the branch tip, the repair commits the corrected file so it cannot travel
   119	  onward through Ship into `develop`. You will see this subject in your
   120	  history:
   121	
   122	  ```
   123	  fix(gsd): clear a leaked auto-chain flag before launch
   124	  ```
   125	
   126	  The body explains what the leaked value meant. The repair is also recorded as
   127	  an `auto_chain_flag_repaired` entry in `.devflow/events.jsonl`.
   128	
   129	- **The repair declines to commit if you edited that file too.** If
   130	  `.planning/config.json` carries changes of yours beyond the flag, DevFlow
   131	  clears the flag in your working tree, refuses to make the commit, and says so
   132	  loudly. It will not sweep your edit into a commit you did not ask for.
   133	
   134	## Known limitations
   135	
   136	Two, and neither is fixed by this feature. Both are stated here rather than
   137	buried in planning notes, because both are places an overnight run can stop.
   138	
   139	### 1. An unattended run can still stall at a Plan-stage checkpoint
   140	
   141	Checkpoint auto-approval covers the Code stage and its fix loop. It does
   142	**not** cover Plan. Since DevFlow's Define stage does no agent work, Plan is
   143	the earliest stage where a run can stall — so unattended mode does not fully
   144	deliver "complete a phase with nobody present".
   145	
   146	**Cause.** The flag that would auto-approve a Plan-stage checkpoint is the same
   147	flag that makes GSD's `plan-phase` chain straight into `execute-phase`. There
   148	is no third setting for "bypass the checkpoint without chaining": upstream,
   149	both behaviours read one boolean. Setting it at Plan would double-execute the
   150	Code stage and misattribute its commits — worse than the stall it fixes.
   151	
   152	**Fix owner: upstream.** Closing this needs a gsd-core change splitting that
   153	boolean into separate bypass and chaining flags, tracked as **G-01**. It is not
   154	a DevFlow fix. The operator considered making that upstream change a
   155	prerequisite of this work and declined, so the limitation ships.
   156	
   157	**What to do meanwhile.** If a phase's plan-stage work is likely to raise a
   158	decision, plan the phase interactively first and then start the unattended run
   159	— by the time `devflow start` reaches Plan with a plan already on disk, the
   160	stage is a no-op.
   161	
   162	### 2. A legacy-arm or non-Claude Code stage gets no chain-flag guard at all
   163	
   164	There is no guard on those launch shapes, because the legacy arm is a detached
   165	shell script with no process whose lifetime could bound the flag.
   166	
   167	**Cause.** The guard is bound inside the pipe-owning monitor, and only the
   168	Claude stream launch path starts one.
   169	
   170	**Fix owner: DevFlow, and as of this release the failure is loud rather than
   171	silent.** Condition 2 of the preflight now *refuses* this combination in auto
   172	mode instead of letting the run start and stall later. That is an improvement
   173	in legibility, not a removal of the limitation: `--mode auto` remains
   174	Claude-only, and remains incompatible with the legacy launch opt-out.
   175	
   176	### 3. GSD orchestrator may block auto-approval of `gate="blocking"` checkpoints
   177	
   178	When a plan contains a `checkpoint:human-verify` task with `gate="blocking"`
   179	(as opposed to `blocking-human`), GSD's own rules say it should auto-approve
   180	in auto mode. In practice, the `execute-phase.md` orchestrator may inject a
   181	conflicting instruction into the executor's prompt telling it to never
   182	auto-approve — treating `gate="blocking"` as if it were the exception rather
   183	than the auto-approvable default.
   184	
   185	**Cause.** The GSD orchestrator (the parent agent running `execute-phase.md`)
   186	generates the executor subagent prompt dynamically. When it sees
   187	`checkpoint:human-verify` + `gate="blocking"`, it sometimes adds a
   188	`<critical_gate>` block that overrides the executor's own checkpoint protocol.
   189	This is an upstream GSD behavioral issue, not a DevFlow defect —
   190	[open-gsd/gsd-core#3370](https://github.com/open-gsd/gsd-core/issues/3370).
   191	
   192	**Practical impact is narrow.** GSD's default `human_verify_mode`
   193	(`end-of-phase`, #3309) suppresses `checkpoint:human-verify` tasks from plans
   194	entirely. A normal unattended run never contains these tasks and never hits
   195	this. The limitation only affects phases whose CONTEXT.md locked decisions
   196	or `human_verify_mode = mid-flight` setting force these checkpoints into plans.
   197	
   198	**Fix owner: GSD.** The orchestrator's dispatch instructions should explicitly
   199	state that `gate="blocking"` checkpoints are auto-approvable and the
   200	executor's own protocol is authoritative.
   201	
   202	## How this was verified
   203	
   204	The mechanism was driven end-to-end against a real Claude agent running real
   205	GSD commands on a throwaway fixture repository, in two arms differing by
   206	exactly one flag — `--mode auto` against `--mode supervise` — so that a run
   207	which merely never reached a checkpoint could not be mistaken for a successful
   208	auto-approval. The run record, including both arms side by side and the
   209	verbatim capture lines, is in
   210	`.planning/phases/35.1-unattended-launch-prerequisites/35.1-DRILL.md`.
   211	
   212	**What that drill does not establish:** it does not establish that DevFlow sets
   213	and clears the flag at the right moments — that is covered separately by
   214	DevFlow's own end-to-end tests, including a real-`SIGKILL` leak demonstration —
   215	and one run is one sample. The drill also surfaced an upstream GSD behavioral
   216	issue (#3370) where the orchestrator blocks auto-approval of correctly-authored
   217	`gate="blocking"` checkpoints; this is recorded as known limitation #3 above.

